#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175814 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175964 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a3e455da-2c31-55af-8122-6fa0e5020404" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Surface by Its Polar Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "52199b9c-bb04-5c14-b4ca-909d46c7f039" :question/math-academy-id "e-8808" :question/problem "What surface is defined by the equation $\\theta=3$ in cylindrical polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\geq 0$ and $\\theta\\in [0,2\\pi)$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty, \\infty)$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\n\\theta=3\n$$\n\nThis equation requires that if we project the vector $\\overrightarrow{OP}$ onto the $xy$-plane for every point $P$ on our surface, the resulting vector makes an angle of $3$ radians with the positive $x$-axis. So, we must have $\\theta=3$ for every point on the surface. On the other hand, $r$ and $z$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, our surface is a half-plane that passes through the $z$-axis, shown below.\n\n![](images/29/2908fb8953f692c596822ecf3fb6454cc5f02f1620f3936d2f5a198d0e9a380d.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175815 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175965 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eae7e5be-e883-5658-84a0-5de5da567887" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Polar Equation of a Given Surface"}] :question/id #uuid "ee821db7-b420-5f37-a54c-a0bbe6cc83be" :question/math-academy-id "e-8809" :question/problem "![](images/1e/1ea30ad7159ada825af44e58f3747f378815e572c0b18d3e8916fd2a6eec58cf.png)\n\n \n\nWhat is the equation of the surface shown above in cylindrical polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\geq 0$ and $\\theta\\in [0,2\\pi)$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty, \\infty)$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nThe picture shows a circular cylinder of radius $5$ whose axis of symmetry is the $z$-axis. \n\nWe note the following:\n\n- Since the distance from any point on our surface to the $z$-axis equals $5,$ we must have $r=5$ for every point on the surface.\n\n- On the other hand, $\\theta$ and $z$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains.\n\nTherefore, the equation of the surface in cylindrical polar coordinates is $r = 5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175826 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175976 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f4472e5-68af-5e8c-83cb-ceab2a6dfe1e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Cones and Half-Cones"}] :question/id #uuid "f948d39d-6d7d-56b8-bb2c-b0a74ac514b7" :question/math-academy-id "e-8893" :question/problem "What surface is defined by the equation $z=4r$ in cylindrical polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\geq 0$ and $\\theta\\in [0,2\\pi)$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty, \\infty)$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\nz=4r\n$$\n\nThis equation requires that the distance from any point on the surface to the $xy$-plane equals $4$ times the distance from that point to the $z$-axis. In other words, the tangent of the angle between any position vector on the surface and the $z$-axis must be equal to\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{r}{z} = \\dfrac{r}{4r} = \\dfrac14.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, $\\theta$ can vary arbitrarily in its domain. \n\nTherefore, our surface is the half-cone shown below.\n\n![](images/69/69978d3f1026cf83019924c44d66f0e510ce8c84b35d10c77c270b87809b30aa.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176181 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175965 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eae7e5be-e883-5658-84a0-5de5da567887" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Polar Equation of a Given Surface"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bbdac3ec-419f-588a-990a-226c34e58043" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z = r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173345} {:answer/id #uuid "cc662dc8-7874-52b1-b0fb-76bc9c5d5743" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z = 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173346} {:answer/id #uuid "0ba7561e-2eef-5188-8fcc-e6ab2621493c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r = θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173347} {:answer/id #uuid "61d2fd2d-eeee-5d6b-9d16-80884c56d1be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r = 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173348} {:answer/id #uuid "6ba093f0-5244-55bf-98ed-0345b5772ad5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "θ = 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173349}] :answer-field/id #uuid "2b8604e3-a8e7-51a0-a78f-ad9606fc4fc7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173344}] :question/id #uuid "217ef9fa-d664-5e65-832a-13cbccc57fc2" :question/math-academy-id "q-102915" :question/problem "![](images/cb/cbebe6f51a470f8ef43297c3e36401bd9c6f97fe05b1a1a96c5438fc87475e59.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the surface shown above in cylindrical polar coordinates?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176182 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175976 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f4472e5-68af-5e8c-83cb-ceab2a6dfe1e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Cones and Half-Cones"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5f594307-11ca-55e6-89fb-478c28a7f536" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A sphere" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236419} {:answer/id #uuid "754cf801-74ad-5792-8ad1-2fb52740f26c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A half-cone" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236553} {:answer/id #uuid "b32fc5c3-4cd4-5f99-94aa-8cf2534b29a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A half-plane that passes through the $z$-axis" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236950} {:answer/id #uuid "e258ded1-3d75-5818-adb5-7e65b8cedb88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A plane that is parallel to the $xy$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237236} {:answer/id #uuid "f7f7af36-9e13-5778-b57a-de403e09e4dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A circular cylinder" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237381}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "754cf801-74ad-5792-8ad1-2fb52740f26c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A half-cone" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236553} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a84cd95-c7b2-5d7a-af8c-9f0819f2c470" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237643}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3e14b0f3-9fb0-5b36-9ab8-66e50f0fd598" :question/math-academy-id "q-102924" :question/problem "What surface is defined by the equation $z=r$ in cylindrical polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\nz=r\n$$\n\nThis equation requires that the distance from any point on the surface to the $xy$-plane equals the distance from that point to the $z$-axis. In other words, the tangent of the angle between any position vector on the surface and the $z$-axis must be equal to\n\n$$\n\\frac{r}{z}=\\frac{r}{r}=1.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, $\\theta $ can vary arbitrarily in its domain. \n\nTherefore, our surface is the half-cone shown below.\n\n![](images/69/69978d3f1026cf83019924c44d66f0e510ce8c84b35d10c77c270b87809b30aa.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176183 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175965 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eae7e5be-e883-5658-84a0-5de5da567887" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Polar Equation of a Given Surface"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aca9b9cd-5c0a-5514-b70a-3484910d2831" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r = \\frac{3π}{4}θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173357} {:answer/id #uuid "b4d708de-3f2c-591a-b70f-f80405f0592b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z = \\frac{3π}{4}r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173358} {:answer/id #uuid "f1a49595-c2e6-5a29-b0bc-b04ce030cc21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "θ = \\frac{3π}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173359} {:answer/id #uuid "4c89aabd-f3e7-5e77-8cdd-283470a56de7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z = \\frac{3π}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173360} {:answer/id #uuid "7fe143ca-1333-56b7-ab47-3dea4ff46d5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r = \\frac{3π}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173361}] :answer-field/id #uuid "861060f5-833b-53df-b3a5-d6ea7eec998b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173356}] :question/id #uuid "954c8b7b-4fdc-58e2-a883