coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{8}\n$$\n\nThis equation requires that if we project the vector $\\overset{\\to }{OP}$ onto the $xy$-plane for every point $P$ on our surface, the resulting vector makes an angle of $\\frac{3\\pi }{8}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis. So, we must have $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{8}$ for every point on the surface. On the other hand, $r$ and $z$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains.\n\nTherefore, our surface is a half-plane that passes through the $z$-axis, shown below.\n\n![](images/01/0118d67477cd0665d4ae5cbebbf3c3d6f3a6a24f4944977d4301cbaf99d3ecaa.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399426 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175964 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a3e455da-2c31-55af-8122-6fa0e5020404" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Surface by Its Polar Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25597751-6416-5ceb-91cb-601deaf1fd9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A cone" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399428} {:answer/id #uuid "eaf0226c-3780-550c-9206-c7ed7218262a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A half-plane that passes through the $z$-axis" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399429} {:answer/id #uuid "3b0aa564-3f7e-5327-a8c8-1edf032ad3dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A plane that is parallel to the $xy$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399430} {:answer/id #uuid "ec9e7d27-4886-5176-9858-61f489c8b0db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A half-cone" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399431} {:answer/id #uuid "fd280fe8-8330-515c-b61b-f9b9b8476f89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A circular cylinder" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399432}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b0aa564-3f7e-5327-a8c8-1edf032ad3dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A plane that is parallel to the $xy$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399430} :answer-field/id #uuid "f35e2e43-0eed-5ef2-9529-98c9c99a49c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399427}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bb65e6d9-45e5-53d5-8bab-c34cad517cf2" :question/math-academy-id "q-104223" :question/problem "What surface is defined by the equation $z=3$ in cylindrical polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\nz=3\n$$\n\nThis equation requires that $z$-coordinate of any point on our surface equals $3,$ while $r$ and $\\theta $ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, our surface is the plane that is parallel to the $xy$-plane shown below.\n\n![](images/ab/ab98ba68465b36fcc3c7ac2de4f50a8f3f16f5e7e10f2c08625cbd933c59f3c4.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364674 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175964 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a3e455da-2c31-55af-8122-6fa0e5020404" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Surface by Its Polar Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "71c382ac-1eac-5abc-95df-6def14541172" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A half-plane that passes through the $z$-axis" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364676} {:answer/id #uuid "6e74e9a3-6670-5588-9023-b750f1f372ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A sphere" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364677} {:answer/id #uuid "eaddfd2d-93c8-5fee-8bad-9d3d4cccb9d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A plane that is parallel to the $xy$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364678} {:answer/id #uuid "7a2bde51-684c-5e48-b2a6-b08aed5f08e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A half-cone" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364679} {:answer/id #uuid "c424832f-b884-5818-8b53-81947b568c74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A circular cylinder" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364680}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c424832f-b884-5818-8b53-81947b568c74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A circular cylinder" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364680} :answer-field/id #uuid "853597e9-85d5-5c98-99a7-5367188bd89b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364675}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f5f2e716-9b66-530a-bc7e-f09e3da3b617" :question/math-academy-id "q-104224" :question/problem "What surface is defined by the equation $r=6$ in cylindrical polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\nr=6\n$$\n\nThis equation requires that the distance from any point on the surface to the $z$-axis equals $6,$ while $\\theta $ and $z$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, our surface is the circular cylinder shown below.\n\n![](images/66/66a12bea76428d4cdfff54210546dfea8b0124c1dff1b4986d7bb6b814f34a2f.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240830 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175964 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a3e455da-2c31-55af-8122-6fa0e5020404" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Surface by Its Polar Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22508e92-a889-50e7-ac4a-4f2293280f78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A cone" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238356} {:answer/id #uuid "34f9df65-d11f-5df1-97c3-bf46d1faaf53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A plane that is parallel to the $xy$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238513} {:answer/id #uuid "546bd773-850d-5702-aa6d-c9ba2d8f34d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A sphere" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238783} {:answer/id #uuid "793f6d48-be0e-5d1a-8609-83b23dde6626" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A half-plane that passes through the $z$-axis" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239099} {:answer/id #uuid "a2f730c7-aa07-58fc-ba9a-458afd976658" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A circular sector" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239474}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "793f6d48-be0e-5d1a-8609-83b23dde6626" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A half-plane that passes through the $z$-axis" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239099} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb1b76b8-a67e-5415-bdac-15fb92d4d333" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240767}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ede99d4b-4522-5a3b-b04a-ad1a84dcbbb8" :question/math-academy-id "q-104229" :question/problem "What surface is defined by the equation $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ in cylindrical polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\nThis equation requires that if we project the vector $\\overset{\\to }{OP}$ onto the $xy$-plane for every point $P$ on our surface, the resulting vector makes an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis. So, we must have $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ for every point on the surface. On the other hand, $r$ and $z$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains.\n\nTherefore, our surface is a half-plane that passes through the $z$-axis, shown below.\n\n![](images/4e/4eefd7f719ccb0c34407d6c02ef1adeb283f45e80ed6a95525661b84e39b3a8d.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399433 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175976 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f4472e5-68af-5e8c-83cb-ceab2a6dfe1e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Cones and Half-Cones"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dad703c3-2424-5fc7-a7e2-7cdbd0fd3e2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A cone" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399435} {:answer/id #uuid "c30f7111-a28b-57da-8d7e-ebcc59c3fd70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A half-plane that passes through the $z$-axis" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399436} {:answer/id #uuid "02bb600e-803e-556b-aa29-aeb145a011b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A sphere" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399437} {:answer/id #uuid "97be7a2c-b317-5f2f-802a-fdce30804bb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A plane that is parallel to the $xy$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399438} {:answer/id #uuid "11005b22-0b1f-5136-95e7-619b8932e0bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A circular cylinder" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399439}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dad703c3-2424-5fc7-a7e2-7cdbd0fd3e2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A cone" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399435} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3e58f04-a3aa-5025-b3f4-1d84092aa5ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399434}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f99fad58-2ad5-52cb-8779-f22e2997958e" :question/math-academy-id "q-104236" :question/problem "What surface is defined by the equation ${z}^{2}=4{r}^{2}$ in cylindrical polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}=4{r}^{2}\n$$\n\nThis equation requires that the square of the distance from any point on the surface to the $xy$-plane equals $4$ times the square of the distance from that point to the $z$-axis. In other words, the tangent of the angle between any position vector on the surface and the $z$-axis must be equal to\n\n$$\n\\frac{r}{z}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{r}^{2}}{{z}^{2}}}=\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{{r}^{2}}{4{r}^{2}}}=\\pm \\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nSince the tangent of this angle can be a positive or negative number, the $z$-coordinate can also be positive or negative.\n\nOn the other hand, $\\theta $ can vary arbitrarily in its domain. \n\nTherefore, our surface is the cone shown below.\n\n![](images/7c/7c8d0cefa5eb8da76f6cba59570670e4078c278c801aeb12344dce3183733149.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240833 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175976 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f4472e5-68af-5e8c-83cb-ceab2a6dfe1e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Cones and Half-Cones"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f451759-2d22-5f29-b03c-d4822bf038e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A plane that is parallel to the $xy$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238319} {:answer/id #uuid "43f32b4a-1831-52d5-b099-c5a8aeb262c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A circular cylinder" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238645} {:answer/id #uuid "89405633-2b53-5396-986f-0108dd6a4906" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A half-plane that passes through the $z$-axis" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239248} {:answer/id #uuid "d7069a28-3aa6-5b8a-8312-628c6022986c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A half-cone" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239934} {:answer/id #uuid "e6bd5050-159d-5432-bb68-a3f588368a4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A sphere" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240084}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d7069a28-3aa6-5b8a-8312-628c6022986c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A half-cone" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239934} :answer-field/id #uuid "2559604b-cd07-5984-a604-420164cbb76e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240395}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0a63f55a-8c70-5d95-83cc-5a9cc3779061" :question/math-academy-id "q-104241" :question/problem "What surface is defined by the equation $z=\\frac{1}{5}r$ in cylindrical polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\nz=\\frac{1}{5}r\n$$\n\nThis equation requires that the distance from any point on the surface to the $xy$-plane equals one-fifth the distance from that point to the $z$-axis. In other words, the tangent of the angle between any position vector on the surface and the $z$-axis must be equal to\n\n$$\n\\frac{r}{z}=\\frac{r}{(\\frac{1}{5}r)}=5.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, $\\theta $ can vary arbitrarily in its domain. \n\nTherefore, our surface is the half-cone shown below.\n\n![](images/69/69978d3f1026cf83019924c44d66f0e510ce8c84b35d10c77c270b87809b30aa.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240835 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175976 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f4472e5-68af-5e8c-83cb-ceab2a6dfe1e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Cones and Half-Cones"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01270b65-2c89-5e3d-8a27-7f7ef987d1f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{z}^{2}=3{r}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238090} {:answer/id #uuid "03bd2933-a4b0-5eb6-b572-2fbf757de1be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{z}^{2}=\\frac{{r}^{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238113} {:answer/id #uuid "475c5a03-b0e6-5396-8f08-cf93a5c51200" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=3\\pi r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238676} {:answer/id #uuid "731e22bc-dbbe-5215-8927-b1f0de4cdaf6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\frac{r}{3\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239040} {:answer/id #uuid "d109350b-0c94-557e-93a8-7f567fdf6cb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=3r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239884}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01270b65-2c89-5e3d-8a27-7f7ef987d1f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{z}^{2}=3{r}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238090} :answer-field/id #uuid "acba57d7-e696-5a84-832a-cd19ebad1940" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240633}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "773ba210-d4fa-5958-a8ac-1084763e8c5d" :question/math-academy-id "q-104244" :question/problem "![](images/ee/ee2764f5cf85f39107e458f347b048cc24227a55fae012b12f6cda4801cdfe78.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the surface shown above in cylindrical polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nThe picture shows a circular cone whose axis of symmetry is the $z$-axis. \n\nWe note the following:\n\n- From the diagram, the angle between the position vector of any point on the surface and the $z$-axis must be equal $\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ \n\nThe tangent of this angle equals the ratio between the distance $r$ from the point to the $z$-axis and the distance $z$ from the point to the $xy$-plane. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{z} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\ \\frac{r}{z} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ z & =r\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- On the other hand, $\\theta $ can vary arbitrarily in its domain.\n\nFinally, recall that $r\\ge 0.$ Since we wish to include both half-cones, we need to include $z=-r\\sqrt{3}$ as well. We can combine the two equations by squaring both sides.\n\nTherefore, the equation of the surface in cylindrical polar coordinates is ${z}^{2}=3{r}^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240849 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175976 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f4472e5-68af-5e8c-83cb-ceab2a6dfe1e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Cones and Half-Cones"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e48d2cc-afd9-55b9-a3be-5b4a4e11f5e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=2r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238177} {:answer/id #uuid "1b927367-196f-5f34-9b14-25b1d5ac52cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{z}^{2}=\\sqrt{2}{r}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238285} {:answer/id #uuid "4268560e-60bd-5972-bd18-b3525e36f760" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{z}^{2}=2{r}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238633} {:answer/id #uuid "4c0a9aff-c1c5-5eeb-b385-c06adcd1782f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{z}^{2}={r}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238712} {:answer/id #uuid "918b0cee-7dc0-5b48-904a-b66eddbd6d84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\sqrt{2}r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239315}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4c0a9aff-c1c5-5eeb-b385-c06adcd1782f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{z}^{2}={r}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238712} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b42dfb1-c5a5-5c81-ba95-b6e3cb14d9a0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240528}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "11d1bb30-6c4f-5361-a860-88f0d5525276" :question/math-academy-id "q-104329" :question/problem "![](images/a8/a89469b49ecb4f40ba8378e1ce5404f5a1cf0eb4e78922529d9dce368b28c82d.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the surface shown above in cylindrical polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nThe picture shows a circular cone whose axis of symmetry is the $z$-axis. \n\nWe note the following:\n\n- From the diagram, the angle between the position vector of any point on the surface and the $z$-axis must be equal $\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ \n\nThe tangent of this angle equals the ratio between the distance $r$ from the point to the $z$-axis and the distance $z$ from the point to the $xy$-plane. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{z} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\ \\frac{r}{z} & =1 \\\\ z & =r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- On the other hand, $\\theta $ can vary arbitrarily in its domain.\n\nFinally, recall that $r\\ge 0.$ Since we wish to include both half-cones, we need to include $z=-r$ as well. We can combine the two equations by squaring both sides.\n\nTherefore, the equation of the surface in cylindrical polar coordinates is ${z}^{2}={r}^{2}.$"}]}
